# CLI tokens via DotEnvUp

Agents **can** run the user's CLIs (Railway, GitHub, Cloudflare, AWS, …) when the project token lives in `.env.up`. One-time setup per project; then use the wrapper or `up run --`.

## Do

- Store the CLI's token env var in `.env.up` (user fills the value; **never invent tokens**).
- Prefer `./scripts/cli.sh` when it exists: `status`, `whoami`, `run --require KEY -- cmd`.
- Otherwise: confirm the key exists (`up keys --json`), then `up run -- <cli> …`.
- Print **identity / present-missing names only**. Never print token values, `up show`, or `cli.sh env` output.

## Do not

- Do **not** run `railway login`, `gh auth login`, `wrangler login`, or any `*:login` / `auth login` — that overwrites the user's **personal** CLI account.
- Do **not** run the bare CLI when the project token is missing. Many CLIs fall through to `~/.railway`, `gh` keyring, etc.
- Do **not** use `source scripts/cli.sh env` from an agent session (exports can leak into tool logs). Humans may source it in their own terminal.

## Examples (same pattern for any service)

```bash
# Railway (example): RAILWAY_API_TOKEN in .env.up
./scripts/cli.sh railway whoami
# GitHub (example): GH_TOKEN in .env.up
./scripts/cli.sh gh api user --jq .login
# Any other CLI:
./scripts/cli.sh run --require CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN -- wrangler whoami
```
